`kill` is genuinely fallible — it goes through the 4 MB command budget and returns `HostError` when the reservation fails or the channel is closed (crates/fleet-term/src/host.rs:319-321, 332-360). The usual backstop, `Drop for TerminalHost` sending `CommandsClosed` (crates/fleet-term/src/host.rs:423-427, consumed at crates/fleet-term/src/host/owner.rs:158-161 → `self.kill()`), cannot fire here: the forwarder thread captures `host: Arc<TerminalHost>` and blocks in `receiver.recv_blocking()` until the owner thread closes the event channel (crates/fleet-daemon/src/services/sessions/host_bridge.rs:276-291), which only happens after the owner is told to stop. Registry removal alone therefore never reaches the child.

